- VC 5 references internal structure 'useContent hook' and cross-compares to output without preview token — restate as observable rendered output on its own

THE FIX SITE (located by the code-graph detective — anchor every criterion to the behaviour THIS code produces):
- src/services/contentstack.ts :: options — This is where the Contentstack Stack is initialized. The options object (lines 55-67) lacks the live_preview configuration, and the Stack (line 69) is created without it. Without live_preview in the Stack config, no downstream component can receive draft/unpublished content or subscribe to real-time updates. This is the root cause — the Stack must be initialized with live_preview: { enable: true } for the entire Live Preview feature to function.
- src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx :: ContentstackProvider — This is the React context provider that all 236+ useContent consumers depend on. Currently it only passes through defaultContent as a static value (line 58). For Live Preview, this provider must subscribe to livePreviewUpdate events from Contentstack's Live Preview SDK, updating the context's content state in real-time when Contentstack emits changes. Without this, even with the Stack configured for live preview, the UI will never re-render with draft content.
Do NOT write criteria about areas unrelated to this code.


Acceptance criteria (IMMUTABLE — the intent to verify; do NOT restate as-is, VERIFY them):
- (none)
Description: [GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS — enable content authors using the Contentstack CMS to see draft/unpublished content rendered in the live application UI in real time, so they can preview changes before publishing. This requires integrating Contentstack's Live Preview SDK so that, when a preview token is present, the application fetches and renders draft content and subscribes to real-time content update events.

A verification criterion states WHAT AN END USER OR TESTER OBSERVES on the user-facing surface THE TICKET IS ABOUT — the rendered email for an email ticket, the displayed screen/UI for a UI ticket, the API response a CLIENT receives for an API ticket. It is a BLACK-BOX check on that surface. It NEVER describes HOW the value is produced.
A verification criterion is FORBIDDEN if it:
- prescribes HOW to implement — any algorithm, mechanism, or approach: "split", "halve", "×0.5", "calculate independently", "per segment", "for each line item", or adding/reading any new field, flag, or service;
- references an INTERNAL structure that merely FEEDS the ticket's surface — an intermediate payload, a "confirmation data"/DTO object, or a specific response field used to BUILD the output. Verify the surface the ticket names, NOT the data structure behind it;
- makes a CROSS-COMPARISON that presumes a mechanism — never assert one value "must equal" / "matches" / "is the same as" another (e.g. "the return amount equals the outbound amount"); that presumes a copy/split. Assert the required value is present and correct ON ITS OWN.
Every verification criterion must be observable, testable, and tied to the ticket's stated symptom/intent.

Do NOT restate an acceptance criterion as-is — express what a tester OBSERVES that confirms it. Address every flag above.
Output ONLY a JSON array of verification-criterion strings. No prose, no markdown.

